INT. BANK OF ARGENTÍ, LOBBY - MORNING

Marble floor, brass teller cages, a mural of cranes against a gold sky. A grandfather clock ticks. The room smells of floor wax and cold coffee.

INSPECTOR LENNOX HADDAD (50s, rumpled tweed, notebook) stands at the center of the lobby, looking up at the mural. TELLER MARIBEL OSORIO-WYNN (30s, cardigan, lanyard) counts bills with an expert thumb. A MAN IN A GRAY HOOD (40s, wiry, gloves) leans on the writing desk, apparently filling out a deposit slip. The bank's manager, MR. THADDEUS PEMBROKE-ADEYEMI (60s, three-piece suit), polishes his monocle behind his desk.

HADDAD
Lovely cranes.

PEMBROKE-ADEYEMI
Painted in 1924. The artist never cashed his fee.

HADDAD
No?

MARIBEL
^He was paid in kind. He lived upstairs for a year.

PEMBROKE-ADEYEMI
Quite so.

The man in the gray hood glances up. Looks at the door. Looks at Haddad. Returns to his slip.

HADDAD
(mildly)
Sir, that's an interesting deposit slip. Third time you've started over.

The hood doesn't answer. He tears the slip in half. Pockets the pieces. The clock ticks once, twice. Maribel's thumb stops on a bill.
